<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahrbaraz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/630">630 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shahrbaraz was a leading Sasanian general under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 614 CE and led campaigns deep into Byzantine territory. He briefly seized the Sasanian throne in 630 before being killed, during the turmoil that preceded the Arab conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz.jpg" alt="Shahrbaraz" width="299" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dust Muhammad / Ferdowsi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Farain_Guraz_(detail)._Folio_745v_from_the_Shahnameh_(Book_of_Kings)_of_Shah_Tahmasp_MET_DP107188.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shahrbaraz is a title, meaning &quot;the Boar of the Empire&quot;, since the boar was the animal of the Zoroastrian Yazata Vahram, the epitome of victory. According to al-Tabari his real name was Farrukhān. He belonged to the House of Mihran, one of the Seven Parthian clans, and was the son of a certain Ardashir. He rose in the Sasanian army and was appointed spahbed of Nēmrōz. He married Mirhran, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, and had a son, Shapur-i Shahrvaraz, and another son, Niketas the Persian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Shahrbaraz is first mentioned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> began the last and most devastating Byzantine-Sasanian war, which lasted 26 years. Khosrow handed operations to his best generals, Shahrbaraz among them. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, who had become emperor in 610, attacked near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 613, Shahrbaraz defeated him decisively and took the city, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> naval access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. He then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, defeated a Byzantine army near Adhri'at, and led the army to Palaestina, where a bloody siege captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and the Holy Cross was carried away. Ordered to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 618, he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> by 619 and the province by 621.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> turned the war. Shahrbaraz was defeated by him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in 622 and barely escaped, naked and alone, from a night attack on his camp in February 625. Later his army stood at Chalcedon across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, but the Byzantine fleet held the strait and the attack failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> then ordered his second-in-command Kardarigan to kill him. The letter was intercepted, and Shahrbaraz, shown it by Heraclius, changed sides in effect, altering the letter so that the army stayed loyal to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 627 Shahrbaraz refused Khosrow's order to bring his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. In 628 the nobles freed Khosrow's son, who took the throne as Kavadh II and had his father executed. After Kavadh's death from plague, Shahrbaraz besieged Ctesiphon on 27 April 630 with 6,000 men, and with the help of Piruz Khosrow and Namdar Gushnasp he took it and executed Ardashir III.</p><p>He ruled for forty days. On 9 June 630 Farrukh Hormizd killed him with a javelin during a ceremony and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boran">Boran</a>, Khosrow's daughter, to the throne. His son Shapur-i Shahrvaraz deposed Boran but was soon deposed, and Niketas served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at the Battle of Yarmouk. A saga about Shahrbaraz later entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shahrbaraz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The climax came in 626, when the khagan, in coordination with a Persian army under <b>Shahrbaraz</b> on the Asian shore, besieged Constantinople with Avar, Slav and Bulgar forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It made possible Shapur I's capture of Antioch in the 250s, Shapur II's seizure of Amida (modern Diyarbakir) in 359 CE after a 73-day siege, and the fall of Jerusalem to Khosrow II's general <b>Shahrbaraz</b> in 614 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shahrbaraz</b>, a leading Sasanian general under Khosrow II who had captured Jerusalem from the Byzantines in 614, usurped the throne from Ardashir III.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-27">27 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the war of 602 to 628 between the Byzantine Empire and Persia, the Sasanian general <b>Shahrbaraz</b> invaded Egypt and took Alexandria around 619, after a siege.</blockquote><a href="/state/sasanian-egypt">Sasanian Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=620" aria-label="Ctesiphon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5691 962 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Shahrbaraz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6141 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6141 1232h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=620">What was here in 620 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
